---
import MainLayout from '../../layouts/MainLayout.astro';

const lookup = `{
  "label": "Client",
  "apiName": "client_id",
  "type": "lookup",
  "lookupObjectApiName": "client",
  "lookupDisplayField": "name",
  "cascadeDelete": false
}`;

const detail = `{
  "label": "Deal",
  "apiName": "deal",
  "type": "lookup",
  "lookupObjectApiName": "deal",
  "lookupDisplayField": "name",
  "cascadeDelete": true
}`;
---

<MainLayout
  title="Relationships"
  description="Lookups, master-detail, cascade delete and related lists."
>
  <h1>Relationships</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Objects are related through fields. A relationship field stores the id of the
    record it points at, and the platform resolves it to a label for display.
  </p>

  <h2>Field types</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>lookup</code></td>
          <td>A reference to a record on another object. Independent lifecycles by default.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>master_detail</code></td>
          <td>A stronger form of the same idea: the child is owned by the parent.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>A lookup</h2>
  <pre><code>{lookup}</code></pre>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>lookupObjectApiName</code></td><td>The object being pointed at.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>lookupDisplayField</code></td><td>Which field to show instead of the raw id. Defaults to <code>name</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>cascadeDelete</code></td><td>Whether deleting the target deletes this record too.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>The stored value is an id.</strong> Over the API you send and receive the
    referenced record's id, not its label. The display field only governs rendering,
    and is resolved separately.
  </div>

  <h2>Cascade delete, and why rollups need it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>cascadeDelete: true</code> makes the relationship a genuine
    parent-child one: deleting the parent deletes the children.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{detail}</code></pre>
  <p>
    This is also the condition for a <a href="/concepts/rollup-summaries">rollup
    summary</a>. A rollup only offers child objects whose relationship field has
    cascade delete enabled — because an aggregate over records that can outlive
    their parent would eventually include orphans, and quietly report a wrong total.
  </p>

  <div class="warning">
    <strong>Cascade delete is not reversible.</strong> Removing a parent removes
    every child through every cascading relationship. There is no undo, and a soft
    delete on the parent does not spare them.
  </div>

  <h2>Deleting an object</h2>
  <p>
    Deleting a custom object cascades to its fields, records, layouts, counters and
    screens, and strips the object from workspace allow-lists, object groups and
    role permissions. System objects cannot be deleted at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Related lists</h2>
  <p>
    Where a child object points at a parent, the parent's detail page can show a
    related list of those children. Which columns appear is configured per
    relationship field:
  </p>
  <pre><code>PUT /api/v1/objects/:objectApiName/fields/:lookupFieldApiName/related-list-config</code></pre>

  <h2>Querying across a relationship</h2>
  <p>
    Filters operate on the stored id, so to list children of a known parent, filter
    the child object by its relationship field:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`GET /api/v1/records/contact?filter=${'%'}5B${'%'}7B"field":"client_id","operator":"eq","value":"665f…"${'%'}7D${'%'}5D`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    There is no join or nested-field filter syntax — you cannot filter contacts by
    their client's name in one request. Resolve the parent first, then filter by its
    id, or use a rollup to bring the value you need onto the record.
  </p>

  <h2>Constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A lookup target must exist when the field is created, or the request 404s.</li>
    <li>Relationship fields are excluded from record create forms for computed objects.</li>
    <li>Relationships never cross tenants — a lookup can only resolve within the caller's tenant.</li>
    <li>For return-type inference in formulas, a relationship field counts as text, because that is what an id is.</li>
  </ul>
</MainLayout>
